{
  "$comment": "Unit types (ruleset v0). The four base types come from the brief (pillar 3) and the movement classes from the data rules. Health, morale shock, run speed, melee (damage, hit chance, swing interval, reach beyond contact), damage taken and projectiles are spec S2 section 2.1, 5 and 6 (settled 2026-11-07; the numbers are proposals, so the file is marked placeholder). Regiment morale starts at 1,000 (S2 section 7). Masses (S2 2.1) are what a unit weighs on a bridge or ice: an engine (siege) weighs 3,000 kg; Fascine Carriers 130 kg with their bundles. Speeds are a march (1.5 m/s), a trot (3.5 m/s) and an ox-drawn engine (0.8 m/s); radii a soldier's shoulders (0.35 m), a horse (0.7 m) and an engine (1.5 m). The first specialists (S2 14.2, settled 2026-10-11) fight poorly (health 80, shock 1,300) and are seat-locked: Spademen defender only, Fascine Carriers attacker only, at 1.3 m/s with a 450 mm radius while carrying their bundles (walking haystacks). Base types (M5 item 6, S2 2.1, D4): infantry are swept sooner (sweptDepthSpeedPermille scales the class's depth x speed threshold; heavy armour, tech notes 5); cavalry regiments are 30-120; an engine type (siege, the Pontoon Train) counts engines or boats in the plan's soldiers, and the loader adds crewPerEngine soldiers of crewType per engine, real foot soldiers who walk within slotRadiusMm of their engine; an engine moves at full speed with crewToMove crew within crewRadiusMm, at crawlPermille with fewer, stands with none, and is abandoned (a wreck, terrain.json battleMadeBlockers) once its regiment has no crew left; a siege engine shoots stones (projectiles.json siege-stone) with crewToFire crew beside it. Crew can't be fielded on their own (no seats). Charges (cavalry) and bracing (infantry) arrive with M5 item 7.",
